window.docContents[96].resolve({"/releases/#release-v4___36___0":{"contents":"\n\n* Breaking change: Markdown in docstrings that are shown in hovers is now rendered to HTML when the site is built.\nThe marked library is no longer used to render them, and it is no longer distributed with Verso.\nSome genres loaded the library from a CDN; these genres now work offline. (#1103)* Breaking change: Manual title pages and chapters rendered on separate pages now carry their registered section IDs as the id attribute of their headings, so links to these sections resolve correctly.\nThe document title in single-page output also carries its ID. (#983)* In the Manual genre, there is a new entry point for metaprograms that elaborate inline Lean code called elabInlineLean.\nIt runs an elaborator action in the scope that the document's Lean code has established, passes the section variables to the action, and arranges for the unused variable linter not to emit spurious warnings for binders in the code.\nFor code that does not need to re-establish the saved scope, the new helper withInlineLeanInfoTree correctly saves info trees. (#1062)* Breaking change: Verso now uses the Lean types and custom HTML syntax provided by Lean, rather than its own implementation of a custom HTML syntax. (#974, #987)* There are more than a half-dozen places where Verso tools need to invoke elan run --install <toolchain> <...cmd> in the context of a specific project, where the cmd is some relevant command (usually a lake invocation). (#1030)* While rendering Verso to TeX, the insertion of paragraph breaks between blocks is now controlled by the blocksAreParagraphs field. (#1081)* Breaking change: Verso markup is parsed by code that is part of Lean.\nThe upstream parser was rewritten to enable code formatting, with substantial improvements to error messages along the way.\nAdapting to the new parser required changes in Verso. (#989)* Verso now provides a Lake script versoMain that runs the main function of a Lean file.\nThis can be used to build Verso documents in the Lean interpreter instead of compiling them to native executables.\nUse lake run versoMain MyDoc.lean ARGS... to build your document, instead of adding a lake_exe my_doc to the Lakefile and running lake exe my_doc.\nThis avoids linking the native executable, which can be slow for projects with large dependencies such as mathlib.\nSpeedups around 10%-30% are observed for rebuilding documents after an edit, depending on the degree of native compilation overhead. (#1054)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Writing Documentation in Lean with Verso\u0009Release Notes","header":"Verso 4.36.0","id":"/releases/#release-v4___36___0"}});